WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:13.630
Good day everyone.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:19.460
This is Jack Van Horn from the BD2K Training
Coordinating Center based at the University

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.300
of Southern California.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.200
I'd like to welcome you to our Big Data to
Knowledge Guide to the Fundamentals of Data

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.200
Science.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.660
And today we're absolutely delighted to have
Nils Gehlenborg speaking on the subject of

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.940
data visualization, tools, and communications.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:42.309
Nils is a Associate Professor in the Department
of Biomedical Informatics at Harvard Medical

00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:43.309
School.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:49.990
And he has researched and worked on the subject
of developing novel tools to visualize 3D

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:55.140
genomic information, as well as heterogeneous
data from large scale cancer genomic studies.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:01.040
He's a co-investigator for the 4D Nucleome
Network Data and Coordination and Integration

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.810
Center hosted at Harvard Medical School.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:08.400
And he's the cofounder, former general chair,
and current steering committee chair of BioVis,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.430
which is the Symposium on Biological Data
Visualization.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:19.810
He's the co-founder of Visby, the annual workshop
on Visualizing Biomedical Data.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:25.250
And he co-chairs the Policy Working Group
for the 4D Nucleome Network, which is an NIH

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:26.729
Common Fund project.

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:31.090
He served on the program committees of several
international bioinformatic and visualization

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:36.280
conferences and held multiple editorial roles,
including his current role as associate editor

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.639
of BMC Bioinformatics.

00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:42.770
He's contributed to the "Points of View" data
visualization column in Nature Methods.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.109
And we are absolutely delighted to have you
Nils.

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:56.789
And we look forward to your presentation.

00:01:56.789 --> 00:02:29.810
[INAUDIBLE]
Hi everyone.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.360
My apologies for being muted for a second.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:39.840
Apparently my software just detected new hardware
and put a dialogue on my screen that I didn't

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:40.840
see.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.450
So let's try this again.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.360
I hope you can all see my screen.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.990
Thank you very much for the invitation and
for the nice introduction.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:58.690
So I'm here today to give you an important
warning, an important message.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:09.580
If people try to sell you fancy visualization
tools that have something about 3D, exciting,

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:19.080
colorful, interactive scatter plots, you should
be careful and have a very close look at why

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:25.230
this scatter plot is in 3D and if that makes
any sense at all.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:31.010
The title of the talk today about tools for
data visualization and communication.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.000
I thought a little bit more about this.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.670
I first thought I'm going to tell you about
out of all these exciting, cutting-edge tools

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:38.670
that we have for different data types.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:43.630
But in the end, I realized that BD2K is such
a broad audience.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.170
And there is so many people doing so many
exciting things.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:49.650
And there's so many different data types that
I couldn't possibly cover all of them in this

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:51.620
hour that we have today.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.870
So I decided, instead, to interpret tools
as tools that you can use to figure out what

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.650
is a good
visualization tool and what is not such a

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:00.810
good visualization tool.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:04.270
And also, how can we use visualization to
communicate?

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:07.080
I'm going to cover those things.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:13.640
And I'm going to give you a quick overview
of this outline here for today.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:17.720
I want to start with use cases for data visualization
and how visualization works.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.670
I'll talk a little bit about how we use visualization
to encode data for visualizations.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:27.380
I'll talk about common mistakes that we make
all the time and that we see all over the

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:29.970
place once we know that they are mistakes.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:36.050
And finally, I want to emphasize how we can
use visualization for communication.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.020
So, Use Cases for Data Visualization.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:44.820
Let me start, though, with the question, how
does data visualization work?

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:49.620
And this sentence might be more than you expect.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.290
Visualization uses perception to free up cognition.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.240
What the heck does that mean?

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.040
So I'll give you an example.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.190
Here is the sequence of human insulin.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:02.430
These are 110 amino acids.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:06.530
And I'm asking you, is there a proline in
the sequence?

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:10.850
For those who do not know what a proline is,
it's a P in the sequence.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:14.480
And what you are going to start doing right
now, unless you know that there is proline

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.040
in there, you'll start to scan the sequence
sequentially.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.130
You start at the top left and scan through.

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:27.590
If show you this and ask you if there is a
proline in the sequence, you'd say yes right

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:30.810
away without scanning anything.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:36.150
So here we're using pre-attentive processing,
and I'll get into that in a second, what that

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:37.150
means.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:40.240
But you can use visualization to make information
jump out.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.840
In this case we're using a color to encode
a letter in the sequence.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:50.860
And it allows us to perceive these letters
without sequentially scanning this scene in

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:52.090
front of us.

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:59.080
And this is an example where we're using perception,
rather than cognition, to interpret data.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.320
And that's what visualization allows us to
do.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:08.090
And in a way, another term for what visualization
is it's an external cognitive aid.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:10.180
And it allows us to augment our working memory.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:15.310
And I have another example here that I think
is very insightful.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:22.650
So when you learn how to multiply large numbers
in fourth grade or third grade, you learn

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:26.139
this technique where you're adding them up,
step by step.

00:06:26.139 --> 00:06:28.790
Well, you multiply and then add them up, step
by step.

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:33.620
So that is another example for an external
cognitive aid.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:39.030
We're externalizing information, putting it
out in front of us, so that we can think about

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.360
what matters and then break the problem down.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.160
That's one thing visualization does as well.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.590
Here's another example of how that looks.

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:49.880
If I give you two functions then ask you,
do they intersect?

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.430
Do they cross 0 anywhere?

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:57.230
Again, what you need to do if I show you this
table, you need to try to figure out what

00:06:57.230 --> 00:07:00.970
the shape of these curves is for these functions,
for two of them.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.940
And figure out if they might be intersecting.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:05.090
If I just plot them, you can look that up.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.930
So your eye beats your memory in here.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:15.630
And you're able to answer these questions
much faster.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:18.410
So that's how data visualization works, in
very basic terms.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.820
Now, how do we use this?

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:27.850
How can we exploit the fact that we now can
externalize things, that we can offload work

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:30.430
to our perceptual system?

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:34.669
So one example is, and I'm going to use a
quote here that I think almost everyone has

00:07:34.669 --> 00:07:40.320
said at some point in their life, I believe
it when I see it.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.930
And we don't know who really came up with
that first, but it's certainly an important

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:44.930
question.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:49.320
And I want to give an example why that matters
or why that is related to data we [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:53.979
So here's something that many of you probably
have seen before or know about.

00:07:53.979 --> 00:07:56.270
And that is Anscombe's Quartet.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:08:03.940
Anscombe's Quartet is a set of four functions
that upon initial inspection with some simple

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:07.570
summary statistics appear to be identical.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:12.040
All four of them have the same mean for X,
the same variance for X, the same mean for

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:13.780
Y, the same variance for Y.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:15.980
They have the same correlation between X and
Y.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:21.710
And they also have the same linear regression
line.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:22.710
This data set is small.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.949
By looking at the numbers, you can see that
they are not the same.

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:32.769
But imagine this was a data set with hundreds
or thousands or hundreds of thousands or millions

00:08:32.769 --> 00:08:33.769
of rows.

00:08:33.769 --> 00:08:36.229
You would think you have two or four identical
datasets.

00:08:36.229 --> 00:08:39.740
Maybe they're not sorted the same way if they
look different at the top.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:42.500
Let's plot the data and see what happens.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:44.130
Here is Anscombe's Quartet plotted.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:48.260
And we can see very clearly that these functions
are not the same.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:54.760
And this is an example of using visualization
to confirm an assumption that we have about

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:55.760
data.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:02.490
And the trick here is that visualization can
be used in a way where it's completely unbiased

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:08.940
to any underlying assumptions or models that
you might be applying here when you do statistics.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:15.180
And this is why we can really explore data
and look at the data in this way and hopefully

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:19.480
not make mistakes like we would have made
if we have just used summary statistics.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:25.710
And this is thanks to Francis Anscombe, who
was a statistician.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:34.420
Another use for data visualization is when
you ask yourself, well, are there any patterns

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:36.000
in my data?

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.670
And that is where we're using data visualization
as an exploratory tool to generate hypotheses.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:47.600
And we will visualize data and then start
looking for patterns such as trends, or gaps

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.990
in the data, outliers, or clusters.

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:52.510
And then start to reason about why these things
are there.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:53.660
Why is there a gap in the data?

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:56.980
Did our measurement device not work?

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:58.610
Or is this really something that happened?

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:01.560
Was there just no signal during that time?

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:05.100
So this allows us to generate hypotheses.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:10.000
And then once you have a hypothesis, the next
step is to try to come up with statistical

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.550
or computational approaches to follow up on
them, particularly in large data sets.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:16.950
But this is the first step.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:23.890
When we're doing data during research, we
have to come up with hypotheses after we have

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:24.890
generated data.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.450
And that's why data visualization is so important.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.250
And it's getting so much more attention these
days.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:30.970
Because we need to come up with good hypotheses.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:33.610
We're not collecting data just to test the
hypotheses.

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:34.770
We just start with the data.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:37.580
And we need, as a first step, to come up
with new hypotheses.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:41.660
And so that's why we are really interested
in having good visualization approaches.

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:45.950
Finally, there is a third use case for data
visualization.

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:54.460
And that is nicely summarized by this quote
by Napoleon, "A good sketch is better than

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:55.520
a long speech."

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:59.080
It's also known as, a picture is worth a thousand
words.

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:05.180
And there is a wonderful example for what
that could mean.

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:06.180
And it also involves Napoleon.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.390
It's Napoleon's biggest defeat.

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:09.870
So this is a little bit ironic.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:12.959
Napoleon's march in Moscow, I'm sure many
of you have seen this.

00:11:12.959 --> 00:11:16.960
This is an infographic by someone called Charles
Menard.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:22.150
He lived two centuries ago.

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:28.899
And he created these figures based on data.

00:11:28.899 --> 00:11:33.750
And in this example, you can see the size
of Napoleon's army marching towards Moscow.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:38.370
It's the light brown line or orange line that's
getting smaller and smaller.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:40.270
And then the way back, when they were retreating,
the black line gets even smaller.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:43.061
And there are interesting things you can observe
in here.

00:11:43.061 --> 00:11:50.900
The size of the army somewhat correlated with
the temperature dropping as well as situations

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:52.230
where they have to cross rivers.

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.410
That always leads to large losses.

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:55.970
And this figure is pretty famous.

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:59.529
So I'm not going to go into much more detail.

00:11:59.529 --> 00:12:31.220
Now visualization use-
[AUDIO OUT]

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.800
And there are also different kind of situations
in which we use visualization for these things.

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:39.660
For example, so we can always go back and
forth between all three options.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.930
And this is, in fact, what we do all the time.

00:12:41.930 --> 00:12:45.980
But we also need to be aware that, for example,
when you're looking at confirmation-exploration,

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:49.399
this is a dialogue between a computer and
an analyst.

00:12:49.399 --> 00:12:53.890
But when you present data, you have a dialogue
between an analyst and an audience.

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:55.290
So there are some differences.

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:57.250
We would use different ways of looking at
the data.

00:12:57.250 --> 00:13:00.120
We have different needs for our visualizations.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:02.240
And I'm going to get into that.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:05.980
And we'll come back to this at the very end
of the presentation.

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:09.950
So to begin with, if we want to visualize
data, we need to somehow take the values in

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:14.270
the data set and turn them into something
visual, something graphic.

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:19.830
And this is the step where we encode data
for visual representation.

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:23.860
And I want to give you an example of why we
really need to think about how to do this

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:25.459
and why that matters.

00:13:25.459 --> 00:13:32.750
And so there is a wonderful paper by a colleague
of mine, Michelle Borken, one of my colleagues

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:33.750
at Harvard.

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:40.279
She studied visualization of arteries in the
heart to diagnose heart disease.

00:13:40.279 --> 00:13:47.890
And the paper is called "Evaluation of Artery
Visualizations for Heart Disease Diagnosis."

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:51.930
And the question that they were trying to
answer is how to best visualize something

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:55.060
called endothelial shear stress.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:59.200
That has to do with the blood flow through
the arteries.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:06.950
So it is apparently so that there are areas
where you have low endothelial shear stress.

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:08.060
You get build-up of plaques.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:09.160
And they're dangerous.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:11.740
And where you have higher shear stress, that
is

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:13.730
less risky.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:19.950
And so there was a new device that is able
to actually get data about where there is

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:23.290
high shear stress and low shear stress in
the heart arteries.

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:29.860
And they set out to compare the existing approach
of visualizing this information to a novel

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:31.350
approach that they developed.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.380
And what they found was pretty striking.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:38.170
Although, it may not be surprising for someone
who's familiar with data visualization techniques

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:39.170
and approaches.

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:42.029
So I want to walk you through this real quick.

00:14:42.029 --> 00:14:48.250
Traditionally, the heart arteries are visualized
as these blocks, rectangles.

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:54.450
Essentially, they cut open the artery and
rolled it out.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:58.850
And then they plot it on top the shear stress
measurements.

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:01.200
High shear stressing, in a sense, is better
actually.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:03.459
It is indicated by red in this example.

00:15:03.459 --> 00:15:06.520
And low shear stress is indicated by blue.

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:10.250
And that is where danger situations can arise.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:12.320
So that is one way to look at this artery.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:18.820
The other way is to have a 3D model and map
these shear stress [INAUDIBLE] on top of it.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:21.200
So this is the starting point.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:25.829
As I said, this is the traditional 2D projection
and the traditional 3D projection.

00:15:25.829 --> 00:15:32.410
The first thing that they did is they asked
clinicians, what color map would you actually

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:35.170
like to use to visualize this kind of information?

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:40.990
And lo and behold, the first choice of the
clinician was this rainbow color map.

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:46.080
And the second choice was this option F, here.

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:49.550
So the reason why they picked the rainbow
color map is because that's what we see all

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:50.550
the time.

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:51.550
We know what that means.

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:52.550
Fair enough.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:53.720
So let's go with this.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:59.220
They also explored the second choice, which
was this color map which had for low values,

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:00.220
bright reds.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:06.860
And then it goes to black and white for high
shear stress regions.

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:08.100
That was the first thing that they did.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:16.139
So for example, what you can do now is you
can map the new color map on top of a 3D model.

00:16:16.139 --> 00:16:22.990
And as you might know, 3D models are great
if you have them in front of you.

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:24.339
You can touch them.

00:16:24.339 --> 00:16:27.040
It's hard sometimes to interact with them
when you're on a computer screen because that

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:28.959
screen is two dimensional.

00:16:28.959 --> 00:16:35.220
So in order to see all sides of this model,
we have to rotate it.

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:37.472
We have to zoom in and out, potentially.

00:16:37.472 --> 00:16:40.670
And that's all an effort that we have to put
into seeing that.

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:44.769
So they came up with this very smart way of
representing this artery tree.

00:16:44.769 --> 00:16:46.050
And that's this one.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:50.100
So they create a two dimensional representation
that still has pretty much the same information

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:51.100
in it.

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:55.779
They are using this two dimensional artery
as a representation.

00:16:55.779 --> 00:17:01.149
But what they did, in addition to just showing
a rectangle with equal height across the whole

00:17:01.149 --> 00:17:08.880
rectangle, is they actually indicate the diameter
of the artery by shrinking and scaling the

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:10.670
height of the rectangle along the way.

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:14.370
So they have a pretty good idea where the
arteries are wide.

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:16.139
And we have also a pretty good idea.

00:17:16.139 --> 00:17:19.919
And the other advantage is we simple do not
have to rotate anything.

00:17:19.919 --> 00:17:22.750
We just look at the screen and we can see
it.

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:25.169
So this is a wonderful situation to be in.

00:17:25.169 --> 00:17:29.320
And you'll see in a second why that really
matters.

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:31.679
So here's a new visual representation.

00:17:31.679 --> 00:17:41.610
And they asked a set of about 20 medical students
to identify problematic areas where there's

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:49.669
low shear stress or regions that indicate
that this patient is at risk of heart disease

00:17:49.669 --> 00:17:52.760
and serious, potentially fatal problems.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:56.380
They asked them to look at four different
types of visualizations.

00:17:56.380 --> 00:18:01.700
The one that I'm showing you right now, this
2D representation of this novel color map,

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:05.770
they asked them to look at the same 2D representation
with the rainbow color map.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:09.820
And they also asked them to look at the 3D
projection with the rainbow color map and

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:14.130
with the new color map that I'm showing you
here.

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:23.420
And the results were striking when we look
at the percentage of areas of low endothelial

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:28.090
shear stress that the medical students identified.

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:36.260
When you compare the 2D maps between the two
color maps-- so the new color map is a darker

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:42.340
bar, the lighter bar is the rainbow color
map-- when you compare these two, you see

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:43.340
there's a difference.

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:51.929
91% of all low ESS areas that were identified
in the 2D representation with the diverging

00:18:51.929 --> 00:18:53.330
color map.

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:57.920
And only 62% were identified in the 2D representation
with the rainbow color.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:02.799
And it gets even worse when we look at the
3D model with the two different color maps.

00:19:02.799 --> 00:19:09.340
71% of all low ESS regions identified with
the 3D model and the diverging color map.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:15.320
And only 39% percent were identified with
the 3D model and the rainbow color map, which

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:18.430
is what clinicians have been using.

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:23.679
So as you see, there are statistically significant
differences in terms of the ability of these

00:19:23.679 --> 00:19:27.080
clinicians or medical students to identify
these areas.

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:30.820
And
Now, the work that Michelle Borkin and her

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:37.570
colleagues presented there is based on a solid
understanding of what utilizations work and

00:19:37.570 --> 00:19:38.570
which ones don't work.

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:42.490
And that's why we can see that there are indeed
big differences.

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:47.190
If you do your job well when it comes to visualization,
it matters.

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:52.401
So this is my motivation to tell you a bit
more about how we can encode data and why

00:19:52.401 --> 00:19:57.220
it really matters to get this right.

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:59.290
Here is a very simple taxonomy of data.

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:01.610
I'm not going to spend much time on it.

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:02.850
We start out with data.

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:03.880
It can be tabular data.

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:05.851
And we can get categorical or ordered data.

00:20:05.851 --> 00:20:09.440
And ordered data, itself, can be ordinal or
quantitative.

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:11.980
So ordinal is simply ordered.

00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:14.232
And you can have categories like ordinal,
for example, and quantitative data.

00:20:14.232 --> 00:20:18.140
We can also do some algebra and compute on
it.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:21.690
Additionally, we have relational data.

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:26.770
That means links, such as networks or trees,
between different types of entities.

00:20:26.770 --> 00:20:28.590
And we also have spacial data.

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:33.929
So spacial data is data that comes with an
intrinsic position in space.

00:20:33.929 --> 00:20:36.310
Whether that's absolute or relative doesn't
matter.

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:43.400
You can imagine, for example, the location
of atoms in the protein structure.

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:44.400
MRI data, spatial data.

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:49.130
And x-ray data is spacial data.

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:54.070
Those kinds of things are spacial, because
they do have a real world representation.

00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:57.670
Everything to the left of spacial is abstract
data.

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:03.440
And that's where we are completely free to
choose encoding, because there is no real-world

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:06.220
representation that we would know of.

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:07.220
know of.

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:11.429
So with relational data you can imagine--
on the other hand, if you talk about this

00:21:11.429 --> 00:21:17.429
like a rodent network, that is spacial data
that has some relational aspects to it, for

00:21:17.429 --> 00:21:18.429
example.

00:21:18.429 --> 00:21:21.860
But it's important to distinguish these two
things from each other.

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:27.870
And now we can ask how can we encode data
when we have data falling into these different

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:28.870
categories.

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:32.030
For example, we can start thinking about categorical
data.

00:21:32.030 --> 00:21:34.380
We're asking questions like, what is it?

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:35.630
Or where is it?

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:45.110
And we can use a plane and place items, data
points or objects on a plane.

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:47.970
And depending where we place them, that assigns
them a category.

00:21:47.970 --> 00:21:53.289
So you can imagine you are cleaning up your
desk in your office and you make three piles

00:21:53.289 --> 00:21:58.190
of paper-- stuff that goes into recycling,
stuff that needs to be filed, and stuff that

00:21:58.190 --> 00:21:59.950
you really should have done yesterday.

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:03.030
So there are the three categories very clearly
distinguished from each other.

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:05.780
It's very easy to figure out what is what.

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:07.940
We can do the same thing by assigning colors.

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:12.720
In this case what we have to use, the component
of the color model here is hue.

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:17.180
And commonly, we would refer to this as the
color of an object.

00:22:17.180 --> 00:22:19.429
Shapes can be used in broad categories.

00:22:19.429 --> 00:22:26.290
And then we can order these things with respect
to how accurately we could recall that information,

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:28.720
which category was assigned to an object.

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:32.540
The best, most accurate encoding is really
position.

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:35.620
And when you go to shapes, that gets very
problematic.

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:42.690
At some point, when you go to shapes with
many corners, it gets pretty tricky to distinguish

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:43.690
them from each other.

00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:44.720
It's the same with colors.

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:46.460
And we'll get to that.

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:50.740
Relational data can be encoded, for example,
by using something called containment.

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:55.150
It can put things that belong together into
the same area and things that do not belong

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:56.549
there outside of that area.

00:22:56.549 --> 00:22:58.390
I can use connection, which is what we typically
do.

00:22:58.390 --> 00:23:02.510
We're going to think about node link diagrams,
for example.

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:06.490
Similarity is another way to encode this kind
of data.

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:09.380
And again, our fourth option is to use proximity.

00:23:09.380 --> 00:23:11.780
Things that are closer to each other belong
together.

00:23:11.780 --> 00:23:14.679
And things that belong somewhere else are
further away.

00:23:14.679 --> 00:23:17.020
So again, there is a way to word these.

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:22.610
I think it becomes more obvious how important
it is when we look at quantitative and ordinal

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:23.880
data.

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:31.190
So the most accurate way to convey that information
in the way which makes it easiest for us to

00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:36.100
recall what is the quantity or what is the
value here is to use positions on a common

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:37.100
scale.

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:43.410
We can also use positions on unaligned scales,
where we have the origin of the scale move

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:46.960
in space or on a plane.

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:49.070
We can use length of objects.

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:51.460
We can use angles or tilt.

00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:55.929
And maybe you already started thinking about
bar charts and pie charts.

00:23:55.929 --> 00:23:57.610
Area can be used to encode quantities.

00:23:57.610 --> 00:24:02.429
Curvature- I've never seen a really good visualization
that would use curvature, but it's possible.

00:24:02.429 --> 00:24:05.380
Volumes are used very often.

00:24:05.380 --> 00:24:08.130
Unfortunately, that's not a very good encoding.

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:10.770
Remember the PCA example that I showed you
in

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:11.770
the beginning?

00:24:11.770 --> 00:24:17.120
It also used size of 3D spheres on a flat
screen.

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:24.370
Lightness can be used, how light or dark,
how much like that in the grayscale.

00:24:24.370 --> 00:24:26.450
And color saturation is another option.

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:31.830
But color saturation really is not a good
choice for quantitative data.

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:38.470
Maybe you're thinking now, wow, we're using
heat maps all the time, for example, for gene

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:41.890
expression data or other similar data.

00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:45.200
And yes, we are using that, or lightness.

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:49.840
But we should not be using this for any quantitative
data.

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:53.710
Whatever we want to encode or show with these
things should be done with qualitative.

00:24:53.710 --> 00:24:55.400
So here's the ranking.

00:24:55.400 --> 00:25:00.680
And you can also then look across these different
data types and different encodings and you'll

00:25:00.680 --> 00:25:04.690
see, depending on what you have, certain things
will work better.

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:06.530
Other things won't work as well.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:12.570
So it's very important to think about the
encodings that you want to use when you start

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:13.570
working with data.

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:18.730
And I want to give you one example, just another
simple example, where we can see what a difference

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:19.990
that can make.

00:25:19.990 --> 00:25:26.320
So you might all be familiar with these kind
of diagrams where we're using circles with

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:32.850
numbers in them and the intersections of circles
to encode some overlap of sets.

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:36.679
So this is from a paper that was in Nature
Communications.

00:25:36.679 --> 00:25:38.429
It doesn't really matter.

00:25:38.429 --> 00:25:39.549
This was called a Venn diagram.

00:25:39.549 --> 00:25:41.419
It turns out, in fact, this is not a Venn
diagram.

00:25:41.419 --> 00:25:42.940
This is actually an Euler diagram.

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:44.420
There is a slight difference.

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:50.059
In the Venn diagram, you show all intersections
that are possible between your sets.

00:25:50.059 --> 00:25:53.070
And in an Euler diagram you just show those
that actually exist.

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:56.420
And so this example an is not a Venn diagram.

00:25:56.420 --> 00:25:57.650
It's an Euler diagram.

00:25:57.650 --> 00:26:03.950
And when we try to interpret the data the
first question is, what is the biggest intersection?

00:26:03.950 --> 00:26:08.590
And now what happens when we look at this,
there are a lot of numbers.

00:26:08.590 --> 00:26:11.950
So you would sequentially go through and try
to find all of them [INAUDIBLE] this one,

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:14.909
and try to remember which one is the largest.

00:26:14.909 --> 00:26:17.380
If I would to ask you, what is the distribution?

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:20.740
Is there one really big one and then it's
fizzling out?

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:22.100
Is it normally distributed?

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:25.799
Or what sort of distributions do we see here?

00:26:25.799 --> 00:26:30.610
You can try to do this, but it's not straightforward.

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:34.210
You need to look at all the numbers and try
to remember.

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:36.100
Because this visualization does not convey
this.

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:40.120
This, in fact, is a non-area-proportional
diagram.

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:45.470
You can only make these Venn diagrams area-proportional,
so that the area corresponds to the number

00:26:45.470 --> 00:26:47.220
written in the area for up to three sets.

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:50.500
Beyond that, you can't even do that.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:55.170
But the problem here is, even if they would
now look at these areas, what we are using

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:59.350
to encode the area is a shape.

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:04.169
Sorry, to encode the number we're using an
area.

00:27:04.169 --> 00:27:07.110
And these are all areas of regular shapes.

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:08.110
And all these are not the same.

00:27:08.110 --> 00:27:09.929
It's not that they're all circles that you
could easily compare.

00:27:09.929 --> 00:27:11.600
They have all sorts of shapes.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:21.760
And if you go back to this example, area is
not a very accurate or useful encoding for

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:22.760
quantities.

00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:24.470
This is pretty far down the list.

00:27:24.470 --> 00:27:27.090
It's even worse than angles, which we are
using in pie charts.

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:30.360
We all hopefully know that pie charts are
not good at representing data.

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:31.970
good at representing data.

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:32.970
So what can we do about this?

00:27:32.970 --> 00:27:37.409
So when we apply our principles that I just
talked about, how to encode data, we arrive

00:27:37.409 --> 00:27:40.630
at something that looks like this, for example.

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:48.730
This is an offset plot, where we encode the
size of the intersection in the length of

00:27:48.730 --> 00:27:51.179
a bar and in the height of the bar.

00:27:51.179 --> 00:27:57.529
So you can make a bar chart and then place
below the bar chart a little matrix that shows

00:27:57.529 --> 00:28:00.390
which of the sets involved are intersecting.

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:03.270
And I can now easily answer the question,
which one is the largest intersection?

00:28:03.270 --> 00:28:04.870
In fact, it's not even an intersection.

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:08.559
In this case, we're looking at SNIPs that
were called by the four different callers.

00:28:08.559 --> 00:28:12.810
You can see there's one caller that generated
an awful lot of SNIPs that none of the other

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:17.420
callers found, which is MAQ.

00:28:17.420 --> 00:28:23.210
You can also start organizing these bars in
this bar chart now that we are free to reorder

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:24.210
them.

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:27.900
We can order them, rather than by size of
the bar, we can root them based on the number

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:31.340
of sets that are participating in the intersection.

00:28:31.340 --> 00:28:39.500
And here we see, for example, a lot of these
larger intersections actually have two or

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:41.669
three sets in them.

00:28:41.669 --> 00:28:44.090
There's the outlier with one set.

00:28:44.090 --> 00:28:47.350
We're only looking at a single set.

00:28:47.350 --> 00:28:51.179
And the case where we're looking at all four
intersections is actually pretty easy.

00:28:51.179 --> 00:28:58.640
But this is just another example to show that
when we apply the proper encoding, we generally

00:28:58.640 --> 00:29:02.970
end up with a better visualization than if
we just encode the data in a way that maybe

00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:04.450
looks better.

00:29:04.450 --> 00:29:12.659
As you've seen, we often have many data types
and many ways to encode them.

00:29:12.659 --> 00:29:18.330
And so how do we pick the encoding that's
the best, that's most effective here?

00:29:18.330 --> 00:29:22.070
And so there is something called the Principle
of Importance Ordering when it comes to picking

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:23.070
encodings.

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:25.789
And that says "encode more important information
more effectively."

00:29:25.789 --> 00:29:31.230
This is a quote by Jock Mackinaly.

00:29:31.230 --> 00:29:36.230
For those who know Tableau Software, Jock
Mackinaly was a data visualization researcher

00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:37.230
for many years.

00:29:37.230 --> 00:29:42.330
And he co-founded his very successful data
visualization, data analytics, and visual

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:43.630
analytics company.

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:47.250
But he has done very fundamental research
in that area.

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:51.309
And that's probably one of the reasons why
they're so successful.

00:29:51.309 --> 00:29:53.279
Now, Ranking of Encodings.

00:29:53.279 --> 00:29:57.630
So the first question to ask is, how accurately
can the data be read the visualization?

00:29:57.630 --> 00:30:01.820
But there are other things to consider, for
example, when you talk about categories.

00:30:01.820 --> 00:30:03.779
You want to be able to discriminate them from
each other.

00:30:03.779 --> 00:30:09.150
If we have lots of them, we need to know how
many categories our encoding can actually

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:10.150
support.

00:30:10.150 --> 00:30:13.280
So discriminability, for example, is a question.

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:19.720
Before we get there, and I apologize, I want
to briefly show you another example for the

00:30:19.720 --> 00:30:26.600
encoding for the scenario where we're looking
at accuracy and what problems we can run into

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:28.250
even if we have great encoding.

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:34.549
So a typical example, we have a table, a bunch
of items, and we have a couple of categories.

00:30:34.549 --> 00:30:37.450
And now we can ask all sorts of questions
about that.

00:30:37.450 --> 00:30:44.130
For example, we might ask or we might be interested
in what is the total

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:46.450
value for each item.

00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:49.309
And we can look at it like this.

00:30:49.309 --> 00:30:50.940
And so this is a stacked bar chart.

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:54.090
So that works pretty well if we're primarily
interested in totals.

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:57.539
Now a question that becomes much harder is,
for example, to see what is the contribution

00:30:57.539 --> 00:31:01.560
of category 3 to each of these items?

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:07.000
Now we are looking at these green areas, green
sub-bars within this bar chart.

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:11.409
And we need to know which one of those is
the longest.

00:31:11.409 --> 00:31:12.409
They're not very different.

00:31:12.409 --> 00:31:13.620
We can see from the table.

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:17.710
But one of them is longer by 3 units than
one of the others.

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:22.669
So the first one for item 1, the value for
the green one is 18.

00:31:22.669 --> 00:31:25.929
And for item 5, the value for the third category
is only 15.

00:31:25.929 --> 00:31:31.220
But to see that is not that straightforward,
because these are not aligned to each other.

00:31:31.220 --> 00:31:35.330
And so this visualization might work well
if you're really primarily interested in the

00:31:35.330 --> 00:31:36.330
total.

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:38.160
And that's the key thing you want to know.

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:45.010
But if you care more about what are the contributions
of these categories across the items, if we

00:31:45.010 --> 00:31:50.230
want to ask that question, we can now answer
that question very easily when we use something

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:51.409
called a layered bar chart.

00:31:51.409 --> 00:31:55.770
We have multiple bar charts that still would
convey, somewhat, we could still tell which

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:56.880
one is the largest one.

00:31:56.880 --> 00:32:01.410
It's not easy, but we can try to visually
add it up here.

00:32:01.410 --> 00:32:07.860
But this representation is much better to
see our distribution within each category.

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:11.960
And finally, there's also this option, the
grouped bar chart, where we want to know or

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:16.429
we care more about how do these categories
contribute to each item.

00:32:16.429 --> 00:32:17.440
What is the distribution of those?

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:20.340
Are they all roughly the same or are they
very different?

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:22.860
And that can be achieved with this grouped
bar chart.

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:29.120
And the point I want to make here is even
an accurate encoding is not going to get you

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:30.130
a perfect answer.

00:32:30.130 --> 00:32:35.210
There's always a trade off like this one where
you need to understand, not only what are

00:32:35.210 --> 00:32:41.409
the values that I care about, but also what
is it that, ultimately, we want to communicate

00:32:41.409 --> 00:32:42.409
here.

00:32:42.409 --> 00:32:46.020
And so you can't have it all, unfortunately,
with visualization.

00:32:46.020 --> 00:32:48.390
There is very rarely an optimal solution.

00:32:48.390 --> 00:32:53.440
There are often acceptable solutions, but
they will not answer all the questions perfectly

00:32:53.440 --> 00:32:54.960
well.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:33:03.791
In addition to the accuracy of visual encodings,
you can look at channels about preattentive

00:33:03.791 --> 00:33:04.791
processing.

00:33:04.791 --> 00:33:05.791
And so this is a little test.

00:33:05.791 --> 00:33:07.460
I hope this is going to work on this webinar.

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:15.559
I'm going to show you a couple of slides where
we have these little visualizations, where

00:33:15.559 --> 00:33:17.559
we have a couple blue dots.

00:33:17.559 --> 00:33:23.320
And it will flash up several times, for about
250 milliseconds.

00:33:23.320 --> 00:33:29.940
And sometimes there will be a red dot or red
circle and sometimes there won't.

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:35.230
And for you, just try to see whether you can
spot that red circle among the blue circles

00:33:35.230 --> 00:33:36.409
as this is happening.

00:33:36.409 --> 00:33:40.360
So let's just try this real quick.

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:45.760
So nothing, nothing, there it is.

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:48.460
There's another one.

00:33:48.460 --> 00:33:50.830
I hope you were able to spot this.

00:33:50.830 --> 00:33:52.610
This is pretty easy.

00:33:52.610 --> 00:33:57.789
Because color is a property that can be processed
preattentively.

00:33:57.789 --> 00:34:02.370
Remember this from the very beginning when
I showed you the insulin sequence and asked

00:34:02.370 --> 00:34:04.170
you to look for prolines.

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:12.919
So with color we can really exploit our ability
to see certain things without sequentially

00:34:12.919 --> 00:34:14.669
scanning the scene in front of us.

00:34:14.669 --> 00:34:19.100
This is the idea that something really jumps
out at you when you're looking at it.

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:21.539
So we can do the same thing with shapes and
circles.

00:34:21.539 --> 00:34:23.839
Do you think that's going to work?

00:34:23.839 --> 00:34:29.010
I see a lot of question marks, imaginary question
marks over imaginary people listening to this

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:30.010
presentation.

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:31.010
But let's give it a try.

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:34.649
So I'll flash these scenes for 250 milliseconds.

00:34:34.649 --> 00:34:38.179
And sometimes there will be a red circle among
red squares and sometimes there won't.

00:34:38.179 --> 00:34:41.389
And let's see if you can spot it.

00:34:41.389 --> 00:34:47.639
Just pay attention.

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:50.940
Did you see it?

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:52.959
There's another one.

00:34:52.959 --> 00:34:57.219
So the answer is you should able to see it.

00:34:57.219 --> 00:34:59.519
I hope some of you did.

00:34:59.519 --> 00:35:05.990
It is known that shape is a property that
can be processed preattentively.

00:35:05.990 --> 00:35:10.499
And now let's try one more thing, where we're
combining two possible encodings.

00:35:10.499 --> 00:35:13.269
So we can use shape and color.

00:35:13.269 --> 00:35:18.400
And do you think you'll be able to spot the
red circle among the blue circles and red

00:35:18.400 --> 00:35:19.529
squares?

00:35:19.529 --> 00:35:22.839
And for those who at least saw the red circle
among the red squares, you can be hopeful

00:35:22.839 --> 00:35:24.809
that you might spot it here as well.

00:35:24.809 --> 00:35:30.519
Let's try it.

00:35:30.519 --> 00:35:44.209
You probably are disappointed because it is
not possible.

00:35:44.209 --> 00:35:50.902
So once you start combining different channels
for encodings, shape and color, preattentive

00:35:50.902 --> 00:35:51.902
processing breaks down.

00:35:51.902 --> 00:35:53.890
It no longer works.

00:35:53.890 --> 00:36:08.619
That's something we'll come back to at the
very end of the presentation as well.

00:36:08.619 --> 00:36:18.440
[AUDIO OUT] --problem.

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:29.609
So that's something to keep in mind when you
pick an encoding.

00:36:29.609 --> 00:36:33.619
And also whether we can separate channels
from each other, that's another problem.

00:36:33.619 --> 00:36:40.829
For example, if you have two very small areas,
like two very small circles, we might not

00:36:40.829 --> 00:36:42.680
be able to tell what color they are.

00:36:42.680 --> 00:36:44.769
So that's something to keep in mind if we
combine these.

00:36:44.769 --> 00:36:49.680
Or if we have a very small circle and a very
small rectangle often it's not possible to

00:36:49.680 --> 00:36:51.329
really distinguish them.

00:36:51.329 --> 00:36:56.759
So these things break down at some point.

00:36:56.759 --> 00:37:06.690
I'm being told I have to stand closer to the
microphone.

00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:10.010
Hopefully, it works.

00:37:10.010 --> 00:37:16.450
What we're going to talk about now are common
mistakes when picking encodings.

00:37:16.450 --> 00:37:22.779
The first step here is to look at colors and
color maps.

00:37:22.779 --> 00:37:24.539
Rainbow color maps are all over the place.

00:37:24.539 --> 00:37:27.630
And I've already demonstrated that they are
not doing a good job.

00:37:27.630 --> 00:37:29.089
What is the problem with rainbow color maps?

00:37:29.089 --> 00:37:33.459
Well, first of all, with rainbow color maps
there is no natural order.

00:37:33.459 --> 00:37:34.459
Well, there is a natural ordering.

00:37:34.459 --> 00:37:36.319
And that's the light spectrum.

00:37:36.319 --> 00:37:38.709
It has a natural ordering for the rainbow
color map.

00:37:38.709 --> 00:37:42.849
But it's not something that we are intrinsically
aware of.

00:37:42.849 --> 00:37:50.170
The colors are ordered in red, orange, yellow,
green, blue, indigo, violet.

00:37:50.170 --> 00:37:53.309
That's something I have to think about and
try to remember this nice shortcut, ROYGBIV,

00:37:53.309 --> 00:37:55.040
to do that.

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:58.509
So hard to order, it's not clear how they
strictly order.

00:37:58.509 --> 00:37:59.910
Look at the grayscale color map.

00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:00.910
It's very easy.

00:38:00.910 --> 00:38:02.430
There are two different ways of how you can
order a color map.

00:38:02.430 --> 00:38:06.430
Well, you can order them in one way, and then
you can say either dark is the smallest value

00:38:06.430 --> 00:38:07.751
or dark is the highest value.

00:38:07.751 --> 00:38:12.609
And it's very clear how they are relative
to each other.

00:38:12.609 --> 00:38:14.640
Rainbow color maps have a problem with resolution.

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:20.930
For example, because our ability to distinguish
different green hues from each other is much

00:38:20.930 --> 00:38:26.329
worse than distinguishing other [INAUDIBLE]
and even lightness and contrast from each

00:38:26.329 --> 00:38:31.120
other, we cannot see this ripple pattern that
you see in this figure when you look here

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:32.140
at the very top.

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:33.249
There is the ripples.

00:38:33.249 --> 00:38:36.450
But you don't see them in the rainbow color
map, because we cannot distinguish these greens

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:37.450
from each other.

00:38:37.450 --> 00:38:38.479
It has lower resolution, actually.

00:38:38.479 --> 00:38:41.930
Although, you'd think, well, there are more
colors, you can get better resolution.

00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:43.180
But you can't.

00:38:43.180 --> 00:38:45.240
The same is true for artifacts.

00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:49.970
So if we encode a linear function that goes
from 0,0 to 1,1.

00:38:49.970 --> 00:38:53.489
That's a linear slope going up.

00:38:53.489 --> 00:38:55.579
You can do this with the grayscale, very clear.

00:38:55.579 --> 00:38:57.900
This is linear slope going up.

00:38:57.900 --> 00:39:01.339
If you use a rainbow color map, you start
infusing artifacts.

00:39:01.339 --> 00:39:03.999
And it looks more like it's a step function.

00:39:03.999 --> 00:39:10.109
Because you see red, yellow, green, and that
looks nothing like the actual shape of the

00:39:10.109 --> 00:39:11.109
curve.

00:39:11.109 --> 00:39:14.390
It would just be a straight line that increases
steadily.

00:39:14.390 --> 00:39:18.569
Other problems with rainbow color maps, for
example, it doesn't have a natural zero-crossing

00:39:18.569 --> 00:39:19.569
built in.

00:39:19.569 --> 00:39:25.140
If you apply this to something where the zero-crossing
is important, you do not see the zero.

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:31.079
So here's an example of mapping these two
elevations in the Gulf of Mexico in Florida.

00:39:31.079 --> 00:39:36.529
So you would not want to use that rainbow
color map or rainbow encoding to determine

00:39:36.529 --> 00:39:39.279
where the water starts and where it ends,
because you simply can't see it.

00:39:39.279 --> 00:39:42.190
It's just yellow across the board.

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:47.309
And that's why we use different types of color
maps when zero really matters.

00:39:47.309 --> 00:39:50.049
Another thing to keep in mind is, color is
relative.

00:39:50.049 --> 00:39:55.839
And many of you, if not all of you, will remember
the black and blue dress or the white and

00:39:55.839 --> 00:39:59.549
gold dress, depending on which one you saw
in that picture.

00:39:59.549 --> 00:40:04.690
So we perceive color relative to its environment,
to the surrounding colors.

00:40:04.690 --> 00:40:05.739
And there are some examples.

00:40:05.739 --> 00:40:10.329
For example, we can make two different colors
look the same, here in the middle.

00:40:10.329 --> 00:40:14.970
Both browns for the higher one and the one
in the lower rectangle look the same.

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:17.309
But when you put them next to each other,
you see they are not the same.

00:40:17.309 --> 00:40:18.309
And we can do the opposite.

00:40:18.309 --> 00:40:21.880
We can take two identical colors and make
them look different by changing the background

00:40:21.880 --> 00:40:22.880
color.

00:40:22.880 --> 00:40:23.880
Why does it matter?

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:28.190
It matters because we're constantly using
heat maps to encode our data, particularly

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:30.440
the large and big data sets.

00:40:30.440 --> 00:40:35.759
And if you try to read anything quantitative
off of these maps, to begin with, it's bound

00:40:35.759 --> 00:40:36.759
to fail.

00:40:36.759 --> 00:40:38.259
Because it's very bad encoding, as I showed
you.

00:40:38.259 --> 00:40:39.259
It's not an accurate encoding.

00:40:39.259 --> 00:40:46.180
And on top of that we have this issue that
colors that might look different or the same

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:47.180
are actually often not.

00:40:47.180 --> 00:40:49.749
Because again, it depends on the background
color and the surrounding color.

00:40:49.749 --> 00:40:54.440
So these two colors labeled with an asterisk
here are the same color, same value.

00:40:54.440 --> 00:40:55.750
But they look totally different.

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:57.480
Here it looks like a larger value.

00:40:57.480 --> 00:41:03.529
And here it looks like a lower value, but
it's simply because of the context of this

00:41:03.529 --> 00:41:04.529
palette.

00:41:04.529 --> 00:41:07.009
Discriminability of colors, as I pointed out,
is a problem.

00:41:07.009 --> 00:41:11.940
So in general, you can assume 6 to 12 colors
can be discriminated.

00:41:11.940 --> 00:41:15.109
But you often have to have more categories.

00:41:15.109 --> 00:41:17.369
And here's an example where that fails.

00:41:17.369 --> 00:41:19.569
You see, on the left hand side, we have mouse
chromosomes.

00:41:19.569 --> 00:41:22.559
And each
mouse chromosome was assigned a color.

00:41:22.559 --> 00:41:24.059
So there are 21 colors.

00:41:24.059 --> 00:41:28.910
And then for something like a synteny mapping,
they were mapped on top of human chromosomes

00:41:28.910 --> 00:41:32.569
to see which part of which mouse chromosome
end up in the human chromosome.

00:41:32.569 --> 00:41:35.650
And on the right hand side you can just tell
that there are 21 different colors.

00:41:35.650 --> 00:41:38.019
You can easily distinguish them from each
other there.

00:41:38.019 --> 00:41:44.119
If you go back now to the human chromosomes,
pick a random green area and try to map it

00:41:44.119 --> 00:41:45.940
back to the mouse chromosomes.

00:41:45.940 --> 00:41:47.900
This is most likely going to fail.

00:41:47.900 --> 00:41:51.170
Because we are not able to distinguish the
different greens from each other.

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:55.249
The same holds true for the blues, which are
very hard to map back.

00:41:55.249 --> 00:41:57.690
Because they're no longer next to each other.

00:41:57.690 --> 00:41:59.469
Colourblindness is an issue.

00:41:59.469 --> 00:42:02.299
You're probably all familiar with this.

00:42:02.299 --> 00:42:07.719
Many journals these days tell authors, do
not send us red-green anything.

00:42:07.719 --> 00:42:11.230
Unless you have a dual encoding where you
use another visual encoding to distinguish

00:42:11.230 --> 00:42:12.230
between them.

00:42:12.230 --> 00:42:16.440
Because if you are colorblind, you will not
be able to tell the difference between red

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:19.979
and green, for example, in a heat map.

00:42:19.979 --> 00:42:21.450
On the left-hand side is the actual heat map.

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:24.650
On the right hand side is the simulation of
how someone with red-green colorblindness

00:42:24.650 --> 00:42:25.650
would perceive it.

00:42:25.650 --> 00:42:28.209
A simple answer is to just use blue and red.

00:42:28.209 --> 00:42:31.190
They can always be distinguished.

00:42:31.190 --> 00:42:35.890
Color mapping, again to come back to [INAUDIBLE]
problem with the rainbow color map applied

00:42:35.890 --> 00:42:37.519
to elevation.

00:42:37.519 --> 00:42:44.339
If we have a simple function that increases
all the values between zero and 10, so no

00:42:44.339 --> 00:42:45.339
zero crossing.

00:42:45.339 --> 00:42:51.819
If we start mapping a color map, a diverging
color map that has white between blue and

00:42:51.819 --> 00:42:54.019
red, will create artifacts.

00:42:54.019 --> 00:42:58.199
Because there is nothing-- the way that you
map this you can see this at the bottom, it's

00:42:58.199 --> 00:42:59.599
darker and darker.

00:42:59.599 --> 00:43:00.999
It's getting dark.

00:43:00.999 --> 00:43:02.869
And then getting lighter and lighter.

00:43:02.869 --> 00:43:03.959
And then suddenly it's getting darker again.

00:43:03.959 --> 00:43:08.559
So you think that, potentially, the shape
of this curve is something like it goes up

00:43:08.559 --> 00:43:09.650
and goes up and goes down again.

00:43:09.650 --> 00:43:11.390
But this is not the case.

00:43:11.390 --> 00:43:15.529
And in particular, by adding light in between,
you can create heat maps that look like there

00:43:15.529 --> 00:43:17.509
are striking features that are standing out.

00:43:17.509 --> 00:43:19.839
And unfortunately, I didn't include an example
here.

00:43:19.839 --> 00:43:20.839
But there is no such thing.

00:43:20.839 --> 00:43:22.430
It's simply a linear increase.

00:43:22.430 --> 00:43:26.489
But because we go through white, that will
make a very strong color contrast.

00:43:26.489 --> 00:43:27.489
And that can be very misleading.

00:43:27.489 --> 00:43:34.660
So here's a couple of examples of better color
mappings.

00:43:34.660 --> 00:43:39.769
So remember, color used poorly is worse than
no color at all.

00:43:39.769 --> 00:43:48.140
And with this, I think what I'm going to do,
I want to tell you a little bit more about

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:50.190
the 3D problems.

00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:52.440
And that is shown in this slide.

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:57.489
So we are often faced with that, oh, couldn't
you make it three dimensional.

00:43:57.489 --> 00:44:01.449
Because we have three different variables
to show.

00:44:01.449 --> 00:44:03.539
My answer is no, you cannot make it three
dimensional.

00:44:03.539 --> 00:44:07.199
And the reason why you cannot make it three
dimensional and why that generally does not

00:44:07.199 --> 00:44:09.289
work is the following.

00:44:09.289 --> 00:44:13.569
Here is an example, a little bit of DNA.

00:44:13.569 --> 00:44:16.029
And you perceive it as something three dimensional
on your screen.

00:44:16.029 --> 00:44:19.829
And the reason for this is because we are
applying something called depth cues.

00:44:19.829 --> 00:44:21.599
So there's occlusion.

00:44:21.599 --> 00:44:23.289
Something in
the reason for this is because we are applying

00:44:23.289 --> 00:44:24.289
something called depth cues.

00:44:24.289 --> 00:44:25.289
So there's occlusion.

00:44:25.289 --> 00:44:26.690
Something in front will be closer to you than
something behind it.

00:44:26.690 --> 00:44:28.690
So there's some things hidden.

00:44:28.690 --> 00:44:32.910
We see differences in size of the same object.

00:44:32.910 --> 00:44:37.309
And when we see that, then we know that's
something that is smaller.

00:44:37.309 --> 00:44:40.740
But the same object that is bigger is closer
to us.

00:44:40.740 --> 00:44:42.140
We can see shading.

00:44:42.140 --> 00:44:44.809
So shading is when there's a light source.

00:44:44.809 --> 00:44:48.289
Things that are further away or facing away
from the light source will be darker than

00:44:48.289 --> 00:44:50.489
things that are facing the light source.

00:44:50.489 --> 00:44:54.299
Additionally, we use something called perspective,
which you are all familiar with.

00:44:54.299 --> 00:44:59.319
It just means parallel lines that meet each
other in the distance.

00:44:59.319 --> 00:45:04.719
That's another indicator that we're looking
into some three dimensional scene.

00:45:04.719 --> 00:45:06.969
And we're using all these things.

00:45:06.969 --> 00:45:13.119
We're using size, shading, and color, the
directionality of lines for perspective.

00:45:13.119 --> 00:45:16.549
Now all these things are things that we usually
use to encode data.

00:45:16.549 --> 00:45:21.089
And that can lead to a lot of problems when
we try to encode data using exactly the same

00:45:21.089 --> 00:45:25.609
kind of encodings or the same kind of channels
that we use to make something look 3D.

00:45:25.609 --> 00:45:27.180
So here's an example.

00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:30.690
The example is coming up.

00:45:30.690 --> 00:45:32.430
I don't want to go into much detail.

00:45:32.430 --> 00:45:37.519
It's a three-dimensional heat map which encodes
additional values.

00:45:37.519 --> 00:45:42.029
For example, here we see problem number one.

00:45:42.029 --> 00:45:44.290
We have perspective, that means there is distortion.

00:45:44.290 --> 00:45:48.519
Things that are closer to us will naturally
appear taller and larger than things that

00:45:48.519 --> 00:45:49.539
are further away.

00:45:49.539 --> 00:45:52.260
So we've got to be careful with that.

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:56.660
Another problem that we see here is that,
of course, when there's something tall in

00:45:56.660 --> 00:45:59.599
front of us in this 3D scene, we can't look
through it.

00:45:59.599 --> 00:46:01.589
So there will be occlusion.

00:46:01.589 --> 00:46:06.140
Things will be hidden behind other things,
otherwise it won't appear as 3D.

00:46:06.140 --> 00:46:08.969
And so there are many issues like that.

00:46:08.969 --> 00:46:15.700
So it is generally not a good idea to go to
3D, because it will cause more problems than

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:16.859
it's worth.

00:46:16.859 --> 00:46:18.859
Occlusion here, another example.

00:46:18.859 --> 00:46:21.460
This is a 3D network representation.

00:46:21.460 --> 00:46:26.369
So you could imagine this blob here in the
top right.

00:46:26.369 --> 00:46:27.740
We don't know what's behind it.

00:46:27.740 --> 00:46:29.249
We know it's 3D.

00:46:29.249 --> 00:46:32.299
But in order to look behind it, we have to
start rotating this thing.

00:46:32.299 --> 00:46:33.920
And rotating is work.

00:46:33.920 --> 00:46:35.640
And visualization should not create more work.

00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:36.640
It should reduce work.

00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:37.640
That's what I said in the beginning.

00:46:37.640 --> 00:46:42.489
It should allow us to use our perceptual system
to see things and perceive things and to make

00:46:42.489 --> 00:46:43.489
this more efficient.

00:46:43.489 --> 00:46:48.650
It should not add additional manual labor
to the process of interpreting the data.

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:52.039
Here's another example for occlusion and perspective.

00:46:52.039 --> 00:46:54.729
I would ask you, what is the pattern in the
data here?

00:46:54.729 --> 00:46:57.420
Hard to answer this from this perspective.

00:46:57.420 --> 00:47:02.440
So if we encode this differently using a heat
map, we'll see that the pattern is actually

00:47:02.440 --> 00:47:03.869
pretty straightforward.

00:47:03.869 --> 00:47:05.940
That is the pattern.

00:47:05.940 --> 00:47:13.859
It's a big question mark.

00:47:13.859 --> 00:47:18.130
These are a bunch of issues you can run into
when you pick the wrong encodings.

00:47:18.130 --> 00:47:25.220
I want to move towards the end of our presentation
and talk briefly about visualization for communication.

00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:30.670
So my first point I want to make is, when
we use visualization to communicate something,

00:47:30.670 --> 00:47:36.380
for this crowd here and
this lecture, we're often communicating by

00:47:36.380 --> 00:47:38.059
putting a figure into a paper.

00:47:38.059 --> 00:47:42.799
And so my answer to that is, put the insights
into your figure in the paper, but put your

00:47:42.799 --> 00:47:44.609
data into a database.

00:47:44.609 --> 00:47:52.819
So when you create figures you are often feeling
the need, whether this is coming directly

00:47:52.819 --> 00:47:55.130
out of our heart, to show what we've done.

00:47:55.130 --> 00:47:59.749
Or whether it's our PI or someone else telling
us we need to show all these things to convince

00:47:59.749 --> 00:48:04.210
the reader, I would say this is not a good
idea.

00:48:04.210 --> 00:48:05.459
Data belongs in the database.

00:48:05.459 --> 00:48:08.109
You should not just try to visualize everything.

00:48:08.109 --> 00:48:11.319
There's no point in having a full-page heat
map.

00:48:11.319 --> 00:48:14.809
Because, in the end, it's a qualitative pattern.

00:48:14.809 --> 00:48:17.960
Often it can be represented much better than
just showing, here are all these things that

00:48:17.960 --> 00:48:19.549
we did and all the data generated.

00:48:19.549 --> 00:48:21.869
I think that belongs in the database.

00:48:21.869 --> 00:48:24.720
And it is important to really focus on the
message.

00:48:24.720 --> 00:48:27.359
This is our job in writing the paper.

00:48:27.359 --> 00:48:31.630
It's to communicate clearly what we found.

00:48:31.630 --> 00:48:34.259
And even when it involves data, that can be
done.

00:48:34.259 --> 00:48:37.829
So here's an example that I to want to give
you.

00:48:37.829 --> 00:48:42.749
Here are a couple of numbers.

00:48:42.749 --> 00:48:47.269
And when we look at these numbers, this is
our data.

00:48:47.269 --> 00:48:51.009
We can interpret this in many different ways.

00:48:51.009 --> 00:49:00.020
We could look at these numbers and ask ourselves,
well, does it matter that the values in the

00:49:00.020 --> 00:49:03.469
first row are all odd and the values in the
second are all even?

00:49:03.469 --> 00:49:05.140
Is that the point of this visualization?

00:49:05.140 --> 00:49:07.279
We don't know.

00:49:07.279 --> 00:49:11.969
So let's try this differently.

00:49:11.969 --> 00:49:14.309
So now we bend the values.

00:49:14.309 --> 00:49:19.089
What we are really trying to get across here
is something that you will see at the very

00:49:19.089 --> 00:49:20.089
end.

00:49:20.089 --> 00:49:21.569
But now we bend the values.

00:49:21.569 --> 00:49:22.569
We're using aggregation.

00:49:22.569 --> 00:49:26.739
So we have three categories of our values.

00:49:26.739 --> 00:49:33.029
So between 0 around 30, between 31 and 60,
and between 61 and 100.

00:49:33.029 --> 00:49:34.460
Now we have these bends.

00:49:34.460 --> 00:49:38.039
So it's the same data, but they are there
in three different categories.

00:49:38.039 --> 00:49:42.089
And now we can ask, is the order of the values
actually important here?

00:49:42.089 --> 00:49:45.340
Is that what we're trying to learn from this
data, from this visualization, that there

00:49:45.340 --> 00:49:47.150
is some ordering of these things?

00:49:47.150 --> 00:49:49.880
No, probably not.

00:49:49.880 --> 00:49:54.259
If not then why don't we show it like this?

00:49:54.259 --> 00:49:56.689
So we have three categories.

00:49:56.689 --> 00:50:00.339
And we have their size.

00:50:00.339 --> 00:50:03.140
And now we can see if that's clear.

00:50:03.140 --> 00:50:07.049
But probably we start wondering, do we really
need to show the distribution of the values

00:50:07.049 --> 00:50:08.049
in each category?

00:50:08.049 --> 00:50:11.890
We ordinarily just care about the number of
elements in this category, which would look

00:50:11.890 --> 00:50:12.959
something like this.

00:50:12.959 --> 00:50:17.949
So here are four visualizations of exactly
the same data.

00:50:17.949 --> 00:50:22.400
And each of them can lead to completely different
interpretations of what is that message that

00:50:22.400 --> 00:50:23.469
you are trying to get across.

00:50:23.469 --> 00:50:29.299
In this case here, the message that we want
to get across is, there are relatively few

00:50:29.299 --> 00:50:31.789
values in the middle range here.

00:50:31.789 --> 00:50:37.249
So there are very few in the category 31 to
60.

00:50:37.249 --> 00:50:38.249
That is the message.

00:50:38.249 --> 00:50:39.249
And that is very clear.

00:50:39.249 --> 00:50:40.890
So we have nothing that distracts us from
that.

00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:46.289
We're not distracted by an order or by any
specific values, which we can see those very

00:50:46.289 --> 00:50:47.289
clearly in this example.

00:50:47.289 --> 00:50:51.349
So we're going from something very unfocused,
where we could interpret all sorts of things

00:50:51.349 --> 00:50:55.900
into the visualization, to something that
is very focused on the message.

00:50:55.900 --> 00:50:57.450
And that is very important.

00:50:57.450 --> 00:51:00.109
So there is another more data-driven example.

00:51:00.109 --> 00:51:01.109
When you have something like gene expression
data, you can

00:51:01.109 --> 00:51:02.109
So there is another more data-driven example.

00:51:02.109 --> 00:51:06.509
When you have something like gene expression
data, you can always plot a heat map.

00:51:06.509 --> 00:51:08.539
And that's what is always happening.

00:51:08.539 --> 00:51:10.449
Because a heat map is a very universal approach.

00:51:10.449 --> 00:51:16.849
You get a good number of genes, for example,
and a good number of conditions plotted.

00:51:16.849 --> 00:51:18.562
And you have to cluster the heat map.

00:51:18.562 --> 00:51:21.539
You can bring out some patterns.

00:51:21.539 --> 00:51:29.400
But it is not very helpful when you have 1,000
or 5,000 rows in your heat map.

00:51:29.400 --> 00:51:30.410
Because there will be over-plotting.

00:51:30.410 --> 00:51:36.230
Even if you look at them on a screen, a screen
has about 1,000 or 2000 pixels, maximum height.

00:51:36.230 --> 00:51:40.400
So you couldn't even distinguish them from
each other.

00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:44.920
So in that case, if you have that many values,
you maybe care more about what clusters are

00:51:44.920 --> 00:51:45.920
there.

00:51:45.920 --> 00:51:50.619
And then something like a dimensionality reduction
approach would work much better, because it

00:51:50.619 --> 00:51:52.729
brings out the clusters more clearly.

00:51:52.729 --> 00:51:56.400
And I think that should be your message in
that case, if you want to really show all

00:51:56.400 --> 00:51:57.709
of the data, that there are a number of clusters.

00:51:57.709 --> 00:52:01.189
Because then the profiles, you cannot make
them out anyways.

00:52:01.189 --> 00:52:04.130
So that's a solution for many profiles, a
lower resolution.

00:52:04.130 --> 00:52:06.969
There is less information, of course, when
you do dimensionality reduction.

00:52:06.969 --> 00:52:09.839
On the other end of the spectrum, on the left,
we have very few profiles.

00:52:09.839 --> 00:52:14.109
And maybe we would want to indicate there
are some really subtle differences or we care

00:52:14.109 --> 00:52:16.339
about the shape of these profiles.

00:52:16.339 --> 00:52:20.209
You can use parallel coordinates or [INAUDIBLE]
line plots.

00:52:20.209 --> 00:52:22.779
And we can get the message across there.

00:52:22.779 --> 00:52:24.390
But you have a lot of downsides there.

00:52:24.390 --> 00:52:25.480
Because there is over-plotting happening.

00:52:25.480 --> 00:52:30.119
When there are too many of these lines, it
becomes really hard to follow the lines.

00:52:30.119 --> 00:52:33.489
That's something we need to keep in mind.

00:52:33.489 --> 00:52:36.039
The same thing is true for networks.

00:52:36.039 --> 00:52:39.660
This is a node-link diagram representation
of the network.

00:52:39.660 --> 00:52:41.309
A network is simply a graph data structure.

00:52:41.309 --> 00:52:44.779
It's something that doesn't necessarily have
a visual representation.

00:52:44.779 --> 00:52:46.180
And then you plot them.

00:52:46.180 --> 00:52:50.170
This example is a node-link diagram, where
every node in the graph is a circle.

00:52:50.170 --> 00:52:54.930
And then you render lines between the circles,
and then they are connected in the graph.

00:52:54.930 --> 00:52:58.489
If you care about the connectivity of individual
nodes, maybe the circular layout is good.

00:52:58.489 --> 00:53:02.479
You just order the circle or the nodes based
on how connected they are to other nodes in

00:53:02.479 --> 00:53:03.479
the network.

00:53:03.479 --> 00:53:08.420
We can see that all of them are highly connected.

00:53:08.420 --> 00:53:13.780
Well, in the other layout, this force-directed
layout, we get much better focus on which

00:53:13.780 --> 00:53:16.989
ones are highly connected among themselves
and which node is a hub.

00:53:16.989 --> 00:53:19.410
So the central node is in here.

00:53:19.410 --> 00:53:22.500
Pick your layouts correctly.

00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:26.829
And you should also never forget that a network
doesn't have to be rendered as a node-link

00:53:26.829 --> 00:53:27.829
diagram.

00:53:27.829 --> 00:53:28.829
That's one way to look at it.

00:53:28.829 --> 00:53:32.239
You can also look at it as an adjacency matrix,
where we can see different things.

00:53:32.239 --> 00:53:35.959
For example, in the adjacency matrix, you
don't have a problem of lines crossing each

00:53:35.959 --> 00:53:36.959
other.

00:53:36.959 --> 00:53:42.430
You can still, if you're on the bottom right,
you can see very nicely that there are is

00:53:42.430 --> 00:53:44.969
a cluster of highly connected nodes in this
network.

00:53:44.969 --> 00:53:46.890
You can
also see this hop at the top.

00:53:46.890 --> 00:53:51.890
This green node in the top-left corner has
a lot of connections to other nodes, which,

00:53:51.890 --> 00:53:55.410
among themselves, are not necessarily connected.

00:53:55.410 --> 00:54:00.819
The downside of the adjacency matrix is it's
very hard to trace a path between two nodes

00:54:00.819 --> 00:54:02.359
that are not necessarily directly connected.

00:54:02.359 --> 00:54:08.650
And to answer that question is fairly easy
in a node-link diagram.

00:54:08.650 --> 00:54:12.019
But it's really much harder in an adjacency
matrix.

00:54:12.019 --> 00:54:20.249
In communication, we should also always remember
that how we lay out information or data in

00:54:20.249 --> 00:54:23.490
front of the user can convey a message.

00:54:23.490 --> 00:54:25.219
Here we have four things that are equal.

00:54:25.219 --> 00:54:26.819
They have equal weight.

00:54:26.819 --> 00:54:31.780
There is no clear ordering to this.

00:54:31.780 --> 00:54:32.780
Let's get into it.

00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:33.780
You could interpret some sort of ordering.

00:54:33.780 --> 00:54:36.640
This is pretty much the same thing that I
already just explained to you.

00:54:36.640 --> 00:54:38.769
Let's focus on the message.

00:54:38.769 --> 00:54:41.701
So we can use Gestalt laws to convey this.

00:54:41.701 --> 00:54:44.650
So here are two pairs of things that are being
compared to each other.

00:54:44.650 --> 00:54:48.829
Now by simply moving the middle close to each
other and leaving a larger gap between the

00:54:48.829 --> 00:54:50.089
two groups.

00:54:50.089 --> 00:54:52.049
Here we have something that is sequential.

00:54:52.049 --> 00:54:54.029
We go probably from left to right.

00:54:54.029 --> 00:54:57.179
There are four steps, or four time points.

00:54:57.179 --> 00:54:58.859
It is important to think about that.

00:54:58.859 --> 00:55:04.950
In your visualizations and visual representations,
what is it that you want to convey?

00:55:04.950 --> 00:55:11.319
And to go back to this, if you publish in
a journal, there's always a problem that there

00:55:11.319 --> 00:55:14.429
is not enough space to convey it in the way
we wanted.

00:55:14.429 --> 00:55:15.479
Keep that in mind.

00:55:15.479 --> 00:55:17.539
There is sometimes a way around it.

00:55:17.539 --> 00:55:21.259
Another other thing is to not overly use color.

00:55:21.259 --> 00:55:23.579
There is something called the data/ink ratio.

00:55:23.579 --> 00:55:30.369
So if you maximize this data to ink ratio,
you will get much better figures that are

00:55:30.369 --> 00:55:32.809
not distracting but focus on the data.

00:55:32.809 --> 00:55:37.499
And finally don't shout at your audience,
like I'm doing right now.

00:55:37.499 --> 00:55:38.499
Take it back.

00:55:38.499 --> 00:55:40.990
Try to think about what is important.

00:55:40.990 --> 00:55:43.019
What is the message you want to get across?

00:55:43.019 --> 00:55:50.069
Reduce, for example, color and unnecessary
embellishments, like making a 3D effect or

00:55:50.069 --> 00:55:51.309
drop shadows.

00:55:51.309 --> 00:55:58.229
If your message is simply to say, there are
two things the As and Bs, that leads to what

00:55:58.229 --> 00:56:00.279
the outcome is, your answer.

00:56:00.279 --> 00:56:05.269
So there is a big difference when you compare
these two slides that I just showed you.

00:56:05.269 --> 00:56:08.579
Here I think we get a much better idea of
what the message might be.

00:56:08.579 --> 00:56:13.420
And with that, I just want to come back to
my starting point here.

00:56:13.420 --> 00:56:19.819
This dialogue between computer and analyst
is something where we need as much data as

00:56:19.819 --> 00:56:20.819
possible.

00:56:20.819 --> 00:56:24.049
Because we do not know what the story is that
we might want to communicate at some point.

00:56:24.049 --> 00:56:28.829
So that's why we want to have access to almost
all the data in the world we could get.

00:56:28.829 --> 00:56:33.869
But that is not true when you go to present
it to someone out there.

00:56:33.869 --> 00:56:36.640
You start out with all of the data that you
can get your hands on.

00:56:36.640 --> 00:56:42.090
You use visualizations or other data analysis
approaches to come up with some insight, something

00:56:42.090 --> 00:56:43.839
that you've learned about your data.

00:56:43.839 --> 00:56:46.940
And then when you
communicate it, you strip it down to only

00:56:46.940 --> 00:56:49.980
the data that is necessary to get that message
across.

00:56:49.980 --> 00:56:54.519
That is the big take-home message of my presentation
today.

00:56:54.519 --> 00:57:00.799
And hopefully, the different kinds of approaches
to figure out how to visualize data well help

00:57:00.799 --> 00:57:04.809
you next time to try to come up with a good
data visualization, whether it's for exploration

00:57:04.809 --> 00:57:07.319
or for communication or for confirmation.

00:57:07.319 --> 00:57:08.799
Thank you very much.

00:57:08.799 --> 00:57:10.160
Nils, thank you.

00:57:10.160 --> 00:57:15.569
That was a really outstanding overview of
many of the things that people should be watching

00:57:15.569 --> 00:57:20.730
for as they're trying to communicate the message
of their large-scale data.

00:57:20.730 --> 00:57:27.380
As we collect more and more of it, we need
effective ways to concisely present that information

00:57:27.380 --> 00:57:28.900
to an audience.

00:57:28.900 --> 00:57:33.559
They may not fully understand the scale and
scope of that big data, but we want to get

00:57:33.559 --> 00:57:34.650
those messages across.

00:57:34.650 --> 00:57:40.750
And I was curious because at the end of your
presentation you had talked about the data

00:57:40.750 --> 00:57:42.059
to ink ratio.

00:57:42.059 --> 00:57:48.049
And I know that Dr. Tufte has been one of
the biggest advocates for that.

00:57:48.049 --> 00:57:52.670
Can you make some further comments about the
importance of that in helping to streamline

00:57:52.670 --> 00:57:56.140
one's graphics to make sure that they don't
violate that?

00:57:56.140 --> 00:58:00.819
What are some rules of thumb that one might
use?

00:58:00.819 --> 00:58:09.890
So one example is when you create a bar chart.

00:58:09.890 --> 00:58:18.349
So think about, does it matter if each bar
is a separate category?

00:58:18.349 --> 00:58:23.289
You already, by separating the bars, you need
to put color on top of each of these bars.

00:58:23.289 --> 00:58:27.029
And this is pretty much the example that I
showed you just now.

00:58:27.029 --> 00:58:33.880
No, we probably don't need to put colors on
each of these bars, because we want to distinguish

00:58:33.880 --> 00:58:34.880
them from each other.

00:58:34.880 --> 00:58:38.019
That's taking place simply by having four
separate bars.

00:58:38.019 --> 00:58:43.799
Now a different story here could be, we do
have a second variable that we care about.

00:58:43.799 --> 00:58:46.920
So here's April, May, June, July.

00:58:46.920 --> 00:58:56.329
Maybe we care about the fact that in April,
let's mark the months where we had a snowstorm.

00:58:56.329 --> 00:59:00.199
I think it's probably not going to happen
in Boston this year.

00:59:00.199 --> 00:59:05.249
But then we would have months with a snowstorm
that we would need two colors to maybe indicate

00:59:05.249 --> 00:59:08.990
snowstorm or no snowstorm on top of these
bars.

00:59:08.990 --> 00:59:13.039
You really don't include any unnecessary things.

00:59:13.039 --> 00:59:14.529
If someone asks you, what does that mean?

00:59:14.529 --> 00:59:18.140
And you don't have an answer then remove it.

00:59:18.140 --> 00:59:19.240
You should really remove it.

00:59:19.240 --> 00:59:23.989
Ask yourself that question about everything
you put in your visualization.

00:59:23.989 --> 00:59:24.989
Why is it there?

00:59:24.989 --> 00:59:28.430
It has to have a reason to be there.

00:59:28.430 --> 00:59:31.430
Otherwise, it's an unnecessary embellishment,
like 3D effects.

00:59:31.430 --> 00:59:36.130
In fact, 3D effects can be pretty harmful.

00:59:36.130 --> 00:59:39.969
Because we get darker colors, for example,
at the bottom of the bar.

00:59:39.969 --> 00:59:41.509
And they get lighter towards the top.

00:59:41.509 --> 00:59:48.130
So if we really care about the values and
length of these bars, but having darker colors

00:59:48.130 --> 00:59:51.840
at the bottom might draw more attention to
the bottom of the chart, rather than the top

00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:55.209
where the differences are that we might want
to highlight.

00:59:55.209 --> 01:00:03.839
And so there is a really wide range of mistakes
that we tend to make often, because tools

01:00:03.839 --> 01:00:05.549
suggest that we make that mistake.

01:00:05.549 --> 01:00:12.509
So if you make the standard bar chart in any
of the many spreadsheet programs out there,

01:00:12.509 --> 01:00:18.150
it'll generally not take this mantra to maximize
the data to ink ratio into account.

01:00:18.150 --> 01:00:22.279
It'll give you nice colors, for
whatever reason.

01:00:22.279 --> 01:00:24.209
There is probably a good reason for that in
other worlds.

01:00:24.209 --> 01:00:31.589
But as scientists and science communicators,
we really want to be accurate.

01:00:31.589 --> 01:00:38.160
And I think, for us, the need to make our
visualizations exciting so that people look

01:00:38.160 --> 01:00:40.880
at them, there is often not a reason for that.

01:00:40.880 --> 01:00:43.599
Because people will look at the data, because
they care about it.

01:00:43.599 --> 01:00:49.959
It's not something that we need to convince
them, to look at the bar chart in our paper.

01:00:49.959 --> 01:00:54.280
They have a natural inclination to look at
it, because they want to know what you did.

01:00:54.280 --> 01:00:58.199
It's different if you're trying to go out
into the world and draw attention to a problem

01:00:58.199 --> 01:00:59.199
in the world.

01:00:59.199 --> 01:01:04.089
And there is more of a reason to think about
how do we get attention.

01:01:04.089 --> 01:01:11.819
But I would argue that getting attention is
always second to getting data across as accurately

01:01:11.819 --> 01:01:12.819
as possible.

01:01:12.819 --> 01:01:16.150
And I think that is a great point.

01:01:16.150 --> 01:01:19.229
That's a great point for us to end on.

01:01:19.229 --> 01:01:21.269
Because we're at the top of the hour.

01:01:21.269 --> 01:01:25.109
I want to thank everybody in our audience
for joining us today.

01:01:25.109 --> 01:01:30.299
And Nils, I want to thank you for giving us
a very useful guide of how to represent our

01:01:30.299 --> 01:01:35.489
data graphically, and in our papers, for maximum
effect.

01:01:35.489 --> 01:01:42.219
And I want to encourage everybody to tune
in next week when we have-- actually, next

01:01:42.219 --> 01:01:44.309
week we will not be having one.

01:01:44.309 --> 01:01:50.339
Our speaker had a health concern and so has
had to cancel on us.

01:01:50.339 --> 01:01:54.819
We'll try and find another time to schedule
something with that speaker in the future.

01:01:54.819 --> 01:02:00.869
